    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "> 1. vanilla, where dispose is on the same page: write on dispose\r\n\r\ndo you mean write with `replaceState`? I don't see many uses cases for dispose in vanilla pushing a new state on dispose, as if it was a navigation event we may want to go back. So this sounds fair to me. \r\n\r\n> 2. angular, vue etc. with Vue Router, where dispose happens together with a page change: no write after disposed\r\n\r\nSo that would mean exposing an option removing the final `write({ })` altogether?\r\nSounds fair to me. \r\n\r\n```js\r\nhistory({ preventWriteOnDispose: true })\r\n```\r\n\r\n\r\n```js\r\ndispose() {\r\n    ...\r\n    if (!preventWriteOnDispose) {\r\n        write({ })\r\n    }\r\n   _diposed = true;\r\n},\r\n\r\nwrite(routeState) {\r\n   if (_diposed) return // we shouldn't be able to write after dispose\r\n   ...\r\n}\r\n```\r\n\r\nWe'd still need to make sure we're no longer able to make writes after `dipose` call.\r\nWe can either do that here or on the middleware level (ensuring `onStateChange` has no effect after `unsubscribe` has been called). This sounds like a more legit change.",
    "commit_id": "f2477f6f8234ff85128fb9e36f03ad12a08e33f6",
    "created_at": "2021-07-13T17:56:05Z",
    "diff_hunk": "@@ -129,7 +131,13 @@ class BrowserHistory implements Router {\n \n     this.writeTimer = window.setTimeout(() => {\n       setWindowTitle(title);\n-      window.history.pushState(routeState, title || '', url);\n+\n+      if (replace) {\n+        window.history.replaceState(routeState, title || '', url);",
